# The pre-write probe is minted per attempt and carries that attempt id as a
# SQL literal. It is transient by construction: committing one would invite
# re-running last week's probe, which is the thing the freshness gate exists to
# refuse. The ATTEMPT LEDGER beside it is the durable record and IS committed.
artifacts/hosted-chain-pre-write-probe.sql
# COMMIT 5.4. The same reasoning, for the two probes T1 additionally needs and
# for the remediation's own witness probe. All three carry an attempt id as a
# SQL literal and are rewritten by the next `open`.
artifacts/hosted-chain-t1-remediation-probe.sql
artifacts/hosted-chain-t1-prechain-probe.sql
artifacts/hosted-remediation-witness-probe.sql
# COMMIT 5.6 emitted two MORE per-attempt SQL files from the same `open` — the
# installer identity probe and the guard prepended to the governed package — and
# did not list them here. Both compile `att_<32 hex>` in as a literal, so both
# are the exact class the paragraph above refuses: a guard on disk still naming
# a spent attempt is a guard an operator can prepend tomorrow, and it would
# assert the right role for the wrong measurement. Found by the post-install
# validation gate, which asserts that every attempt-bound *.sql under artifacts/
# is ignored.
artifacts/hosted-chain-installer-identity-probe.sql
artifacts/hosted-chain-installer-identity-guard.sql
# COMMIT 5.6. The per-attempt WORKING documents an operator saves the probe
# output into. Same reasoning one level on: they are rewritten by the next
# attempt — the witness for att_34fd431f was lost exactly this way — so a copy
# sitting here is a copy that has already stopped describing the current one.
# What is worth keeping is promoted to docs/ops/staging/evidence/ under a name
# carrying its attempt id. See that directory's README.
artifacts/hosted-remediation-witness.json
artifacts/hosted-chain-pre-write-probe.out
artifacts/hosted-chain-pre-write-observation.json
artifacts/t1-witness.json
artifacts/t1-prechain.json
# The identity documents the operator saved from the identity probe, and the
# closing observation of the chain. Promoted, verbatim, to
# docs/ops/staging/evidence/ under names carrying their attempt id; the copies
# here are working files the next attempt overwrites.
artifacts/installer-identity.json
artifacts/installer-identity-recheck.json
artifacts/hosted-chain-final-observation.json
# F-PI-01. The posture probe, same class as every other attempt-bound *.sql: it
# carries `att_<32 hex>` as a SQL literal and is rewritten by the next
# `posture:observation`. The OBSERVATION an operator saves beside it is a working
# file for the same reason one level on — the next attempt overwrites it — and
# `posture:status:write` promotes it verbatim to docs/ops/staging/evidence/ under
# a name carrying its attempt id. MEASURED: the copy left here after the closure
# measurement was byte-identical to its promotion, which is exactly what makes it
# a duplicate rather than a record.
#
# `artifacts/hosted-chain-posture-status.json` is deliberately NOT here. It is a
# DERIVED VERDICT, like every other *-status.json under artifacts/, and it is
# versioned so `posture:status:verify` has something to recompute against. A
# status that nothing recomputes is a number that will eventually be wrong in the
# direction that flatters the author.
artifacts/hosted-chain-posture-probe.sql
artifacts/hosted-chain-posture-observation.json
# The THREE ATTEMPT LEDGERS are the durable audit trail and ARE versioned, in
# place, where the tools append to them. Listed here as a statement, not as a
# rule: nothing below ignores them, and a future glob over artifacts/ must not.
#   artifacts/hosted-chain-attempts.jsonl          VERSIONED
#   artifacts/hosted-remediation-attempts.jsonl    VERSIONED
#   artifacts/hosted-chain-posture-attempts.jsonl  VERSIONED
